//
// PROBE: the alias table, and the `.` workbench suffixes.
//
// Two groups the corpus never spells, both preserved because a program may.
//
// **Aliases.** `create_aliases.rs` binds Unicode and ASCII spellings to the
// same natives (reference/rust_multistackvm/src/stdlib/create_aliases.rs:4-45).
// Nothing distinguishes them at run time — an alias resolves before dispatch —
// so the point of the probe is that they resolve *at all*, and to the right
// target.
//
// **The `.` suffixes.** D24's contract: the primary operand comes off the
// **workbench**, the secondary off the **main stack**, and the result goes back
// to the workbench (reference/Bund/src/stdlib/functions/values/push.rs:29,38,50).
// So `+.` is not "`+` on the workbench" — it crosses both stacks, which is the
// grounding for RFC-0004 giving a stack effect two axes.
//
"probe: arithmetic aliases resolve to their targets" println
1 2 + println
"probe: the workbench suffixes cross two stacks" println
0 . 5 +. take println
10 . 3 -. take println
2 . 6 *. take println
12 . 3 /. take println
"probe: lambda aliases build a LAMBDA, they do not test for one" println
λ type println drop
Λ type println drop
clear
"probe: nodata aliases" println
∅ convert.to_string println
| convert.to_string println
"probe: ?true and ?false are if and if.false" println
true { "then ran" println } ?true
false { "else ran" println } ?false
"probe: ifthenelse spelled in full" println
42 42 != { true } { false } ifthenelse println
clear
"probe: the reflection predicates" println
:println ?stdlib println
:version ?alias println
"probe: type and type.of" println
"x" type println drop
1 type.of println drop
clear
"probe: cdr and the distance aliases" println
[ 1 2 3 ] cdr println
"kitten" "sitting" string.distance println
"ca" "abc" string.distance.dameraulevenshtein println
"probe: sysinfo.version resolves as a word" println
:sysinfo.version ?word println
